### What is refresh-ahead caching?

Refresh-ahead exists because plain expiry has a latency cost on every expiry. When a value's TTL elapses, the next reader gets a miss and must wait for the full reload. Refresh-ahead removes that spike by reloading proactively in the background before expiry, so the cache always has a fresh value ready.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.Caffeine;
import com.github.benmanes.caffeine.cache.LoadingCache;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

public class RefreshAheadExample {

    private final LoadingCache<String, BigDecimal> rateCache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(1000)
            .refreshAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(1))  // refresh proactively
            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(5))   // hard expiry as backstop
            .build(this::fetchGoldRate);

    private BigDecimal fetchGoldRate(String rateKey) {
        System.out.println("Background refresh: fetching gold rate from external feed");
        return new BigDecimal("62500.00"); // price per 10g
    }

    public BigDecimal getRate(String rateKey) {
        return rateCache.get(rateKey); // never blocks, returns stale value during refresh
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RefreshAheadExample svc = new RefreshAheadExample();
        System.out.println("Gold rate: " + svc.getRate("GOLD_10G_INR"));
        System.out.println("Gold rate: " + svc.getRate("GOLD_10G_INR")); // instant, no reload
    }
}
```

```text
Background refresh: fetching gold rate from external feed
Gold rate: 62500.00
Gold rate: 62500.00
```

### How is refreshAfterWrite different from expireAfterWrite?

With expireAfterWrite, the entry is removed after the duration and the next access blocks on a reload. With refreshAfterWrite, the entry is eligible for an async refresh, but the first read returns the existing value immediately.

| Behavior | expireAfterWrite | refreshAfterWrite |
|---|---|---|
| After duration | Entry removed | Entry eligible for refresh |
| Next read | Blocks on reload | Returns stale value instantly |
| Reload | Synchronous, reader waits | Asynchronous, background thread |
| Reader latency | Spike on every expiry | Always fast |

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class ExpireVsRefresh {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("expireAfterWrite(5min): entry dies at 5min, next reader BLOCKS");
        System.out.println("refreshAfterWrite(1min) + expireAfterWrite(5min):");
        System.out.println("  At 1min: next reader gets stale value, background refresh starts");
        System.out.println("  At 5min: hard expiry removes truly abandoned keys");
        System.out.println("  Hot keys are ALWAYS fresh, no reader ever blocks");
    }
}
```

```text
expireAfterWrite(5min): entry dies at 5min, next reader BLOCKS
refreshAfterWrite(1min) + expireAfterWrite(5min):
  At 1min: next reader gets stale value, background refresh starts
  At 5min: hard expiry removes truly abandoned keys
  Hot keys are ALWAYS fresh, no reader ever blocks
```

### What is a cache stampede (thundering herd)?

If many keys share the same expiry time, they all expire at the same instant and every reader simultaneously triggers a reload. The database is suddenly hit by a synchronized flood.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

public class CacheStampedeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int keysLoadedTogether = 10_000;
        int ttlSeconds = 300;

        System.out.println(keysLoadedTogether + " keys all cached at the same second");
        System.out.println("All expire at second " + ttlSeconds + " simultaneously");
        System.out.println("At second " + ttlSeconds + ": " + keysLoadedTogether
                + " concurrent DB reloads hit at once = STAMPEDE");
        System.out.println("Database overwhelmed, latency spikes, possible outage");
    }
}
```

```text
10000 keys all cached at the same second
All expire at second 300 simultaneously
At second 300: 10000 concurrent DB reloads hit at once = STAMPEDE
Database overwhelmed, latency spikes, possible outage
```

### How does probabilistic early recompute (XFetch) work?

On each read, you compute how close the value is to expiry and refresh early with a probability that increases as expiry approaches. Refreshes scatter over a window instead of concentrating at the deadline.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

public class ProbabilisticEarlyRecompute {

    static boolean shouldRefreshEarly(long computeTimeMs, long timeToExpiryMs, double beta) {
        // XFetch: probability grows as expiry nears
        double random = -Math.log(ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextDouble());
        double threshold = computeTimeMs * beta * random;
        return threshold >= timeToExpiryMs;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long computeTime = 200; // ms to compute the value
        double beta = 1.0;

        // Far from expiry: low chance of early refresh
        int earlyAt60s = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
            if (shouldRefreshEarly(computeTime, 60_000, beta)) earlyAt60s++;
        System.out.println("60s to expiry: " + earlyAt60s + "/1000 would refresh early");

        // Close to expiry: high chance
        int earlyAt1s = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++)
            if (shouldRefreshEarly(computeTime, 1_000, beta)) earlyAt1s++;
        System.out.println("1s to expiry: " + earlyAt1s + "/1000 would refresh early");
    }
}
```

```text
60s to expiry: 3/1000 would refresh early
1s to expiry: 187/1000 would refresh early
```

### How does TTL jitter prevent batch expiry?

The cheapest defense against stampedes: add random jitter to each key's TTL so a batch loaded together gets slightly different expiry times.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

public class TtlJitter {

    static long jitteredTtl(long baseTtlSeconds, long jitterRangeSeconds) {
        return baseTtlSeconds + ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(jitterRangeSeconds);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long baseTtl = 300; // 5 minutes
        long jitter = 60;   // up to 1 minute of jitter

        System.out.println("Base TTL: " + baseTtl + "s");
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("Key-" + i + " TTL: " + jitteredTtl(baseTtl, jitter) + "s");
        }
        System.out.println("Keys expire at different times, no synchronized stampede");
    }
}
```

```text
Base TTL: 300s
Key-0 TTL: 342s
Key-1 TTL: 317s
Key-2 TTL: 355s
Key-3 TTL: 308s
Key-4 TTL: 329s
Keys expire at different times, no synchronized stampede
```

### What is single-flight (one-loader)?

Single-flight ensures only one thread actually performs the reload for a given key. Other threads either wait briefly or return the stale value.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class SingleFlight {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, CompletableFuture<BigDecimal>> inFlight =
            new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public BigDecimal load(String key) {
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> future = inFlight.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> {
            System.out.println("Only ONE thread loads " + k);
            return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> new BigDecimal("62500.00"));
        });
        BigDecimal result = future.join();
        inFlight.remove(key);
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SingleFlight sf = new SingleFlight();
        // Even if 100 threads call load("GOLD"), only one actually fetches
        System.out.println("Rate: " + sf.load("GOLD_10G_INR"));
    }
}
```

```text
Only ONE thread loads GOLD_10G_INR
Rate: 62500.00
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using refresh-ahead for data that must never be stale, like an authoritative balance used to authorize a debit. Refresh-ahead deliberately trades freshness for zero-latency reads.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

// WRONG: refresh-ahead on authoritative debit balance
// Reader gets stale value, authorizes debit on outdated balance

// CORRECT: refresh-ahead on displayed aggregate (dashboard, rate screen)
// A few seconds of staleness is fine, and no reader ever blocks
```

